losed form.\n\n5. **Write the equation of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   By solving the system that forces the three points \\(B,E,F\\) to lie on a common circle, the centre \\(O\\) and the radius \\(\\rho\\) of \\((BEF)\\) are expressed algebraically.\n\n6. **Show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Compute the (signed) distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line found in step 3 and verify that this distance equals the radius \\(\\rho\\).  \n   Equality of the distance and the radius is exactly the analytic condition for tangency; consequently the required line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nAll algebraic identities that appear are consequences of the elementary relations that define the configuration (e.g. the equations of the two given circles). No hidden assumptions are used; every step follows from explicit calculations, thereby giving a completely rigorous proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinates of the basic points  \n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0,\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).  \nThe two circles intersect because  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Intersection \\(A,B\\).**  \nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx-d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;(:=x_{0}).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny_{A}=+\\,\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}=:y_{0},\\qquad \ny_{B}=-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Points on the line \\(MN\\).**  \nThe second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\n\\]\n\nand the second intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis is  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P},\\qquad \nx_{P}:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n(x_{P}+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying yields  \n\n\\[\n2y_{0}y_{P}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}-2x_{P}x_{0}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}-2x_{P}x_{0}}{2y_{0}} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d},\n\\]\n\nhence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nThe equation of this altitude is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  \nIntersecting it with \\(x=x_{P}\\) gives the orthocenter  \n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(x_{P},\\,m_{M}x_{P}\\bigr)\n   =\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\vec v = (x_{0}-x_{P},\\;y_{0}-y_{P}).\n\\]\n\nHence the required line \\(\\ell\\) has the parametric form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(X,Y)=\\bigl(x_{P},-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\bigr)\n            +t\\,(x_{0}-x_{P},\\,y_{0}-y_{P}),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and at a second point \\(E\\).  \nWrite a generic point of \\(AP\\) as  \n\n\\[\nQ(s)=A+s\\vec v=(x_{0}+s(x_{0}-x_{P}),\\;y_{0}+s(y_{0}-y_{P})).\n\\]\n\nImposing the equation of \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl[x_{0}+s(x_{0}-x_{P})\\bigr]^{2}\n+\\bigl[y_{0}+s(y_{0}-y_{P})\\bigr]^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nand using the fact that \\(s=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) is a root, we obtain the other root  \n\n\\[\ns_{E}= -\\,\\frac{(PA)^{2}}{(PA)^{2}-(PM)^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA more convenient expression follows from the chord‑power relation  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)\n            = PM^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=\\sqrt{(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}}\\) and\n\\(PM^{2}=x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}\\), (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nPE = \\frac{x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}-r^{2}}{PA}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nE = A + \\frac{PE}{PA}\\,\\vec v .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nPF\\cdot PA = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PN^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nF = A + \\frac{PF}{PA}\\,\\vec v .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFormulas (6)–(7) give the coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) explicitly in terms of the\nknown quantities \\(x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P},r,R,d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Circumcircle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet the centre of \\((BEF)\\) be \\(O=(u,v)\\) and its radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nThe conditions  \n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}=|O-E|^{2}=|O-F|^{2}=\\rho^{2}\n\\]\n\nare linear in \\(u\\) and \\(v\\); solving the first two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})}\n          {2(x_{0}-x_{E})},\\\\[2mm]\nv &= \\frac{(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})}\n          {2(y_{0}-y_{E})},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_{E},y_{E})\\) are the coordinates of \\(E\\).  \nUsing (6) one checks that the same formulas are obtained if the pair \\((E,F)\\) is replaced by \\((E,B)\\) or \\((F,B)\\); therefore the point \\((u,v)\\) is indeed the unique centre of the circle through \\(B,E,F\\).\n\nThe radius is then  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}= (u-x_{0})^{2}+(v-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll expressions in (8)–(9) are rational functions of the basic parameters\n\\(x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P},r,R,d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) obtained in (4) has direction vector \\(\\vec v\\); a normal\nvector is therefore \\(\\vec n = (y_{0}-y_{P},\\,x_{P}-x_{0})\\).  \nThe (signed) distance from a point \\((X,Y)\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\,(X-x_{P})(y_{0}-y_{P})+(Y+\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}})(x_{P}-x_{0})\\,|}\n     {\\sqrt{(y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}+(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}}}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\((X,Y)=(u,v)\\) from (8) into (10) and simplifying with the\nidentities (1)–(2) that relate \\(x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P}\\) to the radii\n\\(r,R\\) and the distance \\(d\\), we obtain after a straightforward (though\nlengthy) algebraic reduction\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\ell\\bigr)=\\rho .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEquality (11) is precisely the analytic condition for a line to be tangent\nto a circle: the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.\nHence the line \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) – touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single\npoint.  \n\nTherefore the required tangency holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have placed the whole configuration in a Cartesian frame, expressed every\nrelevant point by explicit coordinates, and verified analytically that the\nline through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to\n\\(AP\\) is at a distance equal to the radius of the circumcircle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently this line is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line