ius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have successfully solved the problem.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Coordinate set‑up.** Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n   \\]\n   Let the radii of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) with \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\) (so the circles intersect in two points).\n\n2. **Intersection points.** Solving the two circle equations gives  \n   \\[\n   A=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad B=\\bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \n   x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\; y_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points on the line \\(MN\\).**  \n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Centre \\(P\\) of \\((ACD)\\).**  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\) with  \n   \\[\n   x_{P}= \\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Using the condition \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,y_{P}= -\\dfrac{x_{P}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \n   The slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); hence the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-1/m_{PN}\\) and equation \\(y=-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x\\).  \n   Their intersection gives  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,H=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\dfrac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr)\\,}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   The direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\vec v=(x_{0}-x_{P},\\,y_{0}-y_{P})\\).  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\ell:\\;(X,Y)=\\bigl(x_{P},-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\bigr)\n               +t\\,(x_{0}-x_{P},\\,y_{0}-y_{P}),\\;t\\in\\mathbb R .\\,} \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   For any point \\(Q\\) on the line \\(AP\\) the power‑of‑a‑point formulas give  \n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PQ = PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\n   PA\\cdot PQ = PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Denoting \\(PA=\\sqrt{(x_{0}-x_{P})^{2}+(y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}}\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   PE=\\frac{PM^{2}-r^{2}}{PA},\\qquad\n   PF=\\frac{PN^{2}-R^{2}}{PA}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same ray \\(PA\\) beyond \\(A\\), their coordinates are\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,E=A+\\frac{PE}{PA}\\,\\vec v,\\qquad\n          F=A+\\frac{PF}{PA}\\,\\vec v.\\,} \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(O=(u,v)\\).  From the equalities of distances we obtain the linear system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (x_{E}-x_{B})\\,u+(y_{E}-y_{B})\\,v\n      =\\dfrac{x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2}}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n   (x_{F}-x_{B})\\,u+(y_{F}-y_{B})\\,v\n      =\\dfrac{x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2}}{2}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Solving (5) yields the unique centre \\(O\\); its radius is\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\rho^{2}= (u-x_{B})^{2}+(v-y_{B})^{2}\\,}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency verification.**  \n   A normal vector to \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\vec n=(y_{0}-y_{P},\\,x_{P}-x_{0})\\).  \n   Using (3) the signed distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n   \\frac{\\bigl|(u-x_{P})(y_{0}-y_{P})+(v+\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}})(x_{P}-x_{0})\\bigr|}\n        {\\sqrt{(y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}+(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}}}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the explicit expressions (1)–(6) into (7) and simplifying\n   (the simplifications use only the elementary identities  \n   \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) and  \n   \\((x_{P}+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}\\)),\n   we obtain the exact equality\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho\\,}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (8) is precisely the analytic condition for a line to be tangent to a circle.  \n   Hence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
